            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Exile and Later Life</strong><br>\nAfter Napoleon's fall, David was exiled to Brussels in 1816 where he continued to paint. His later works moved away from political subjects, focusing instead on mythological themes and the human form.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Influence on Art and Politics</strong><br>\nDavid's commitment to classical subject matter and form had a lasting impact on the art world, and his career illustrates the close connections between art and politics during times of social upheaval.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nJacques-Louis David died in 1825. His approach to painting influenced the next generation of artists, and his works remain celebrated for their pioneering depiction of neoclassical aesthetics and for the narrative power of his historical paintings.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
